  "response": "Competing accounts of the same log evidence are resolved not by preferring one narrative over the other but by testing each against what the preserved artifacts can actually bear. Both sides here point to the same records, so the question is which inferences those records license. The company asserted that the employee deliberately copied customer records before resigning; what produced that assertion was its data loss prevention system's logging of an upload of a customer database to a personal cloud storage account. The employee, by contrast, asserted that an external attacker authenticated with his stolen credentials.\n\nThe first analytical step is to state the hypothesis space precisely rather than collapse it prematurely. At least one of the following is true, and perhaps both: a person other than the employee used the employee's credentials to access the customer database, or the employee copied customer records to a personal storage device; the logged upload of the database to personal cloud storage indicates the second of these. Volume is then quantified from egress telemetry. Fourteen gigabytes of customer data left the corporate network \u2014 in other words, a total of 14 GB of customer data was transferred outside the company's environment.\n\nSequence comes next, and it is here that most disputed timelines are won or lost. Investigators normalized all log timestamps to UTC before building the incident timeline, and precisely because the clocks had been reconciled in this way, impossible-travel analysis flagged two successful logins to the same account from different continents 11 minutes apart. That flag shows that at least one authenticated session on the employee's account originated from an IP address outside the corporate network, and it equally confirms the first limb of the alternative set out above, namely that someone other than the employee was using his credentials against the customer database.\n\nThe VPN authentication logs recorded a session on the employee's account from an IP address allocated in Eastern Europe. VPN concentrator logs record the content of the encrypted traffic passing through the tunnel. Attribution therefore depends on correlating authentication artifacts with endpoint forensics, egress records and device inventories. Workstation evidence such as USB enumeration, file-system metadata and browser upload history speaks to local copying; remote-access telemetry speaks to the origin and duration of a session. No single source answers the question of who was at the keyboard, and a responsible finding rests on the convergence of several.\n\nThe company notified the data protection regulator of the breach. The GDPR requires notification of a personal data breach within 24 hours of discovery. Responders document each inference, the artifact that supports it, and the artifact that would have falsified it had the opposing account been correct, so that a disciplinary panel, regulator or court can see which of the two explanations the record genuinely sustains and where it remains silent. An account that requires the logs to say more than they were designed to capture is, for that reason alone, the weaker of the two.",
